WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.364
Blazor components should render untrusted
data safely.

2
00:00:06.444 --> 00:00:15.241
Normal framework rendering and encoding
help keep ordinary values as text instead of

3
00:00:15.321 --> 00:00:17.765
browser instructions.

4
00:00:17.845 --> 00:00:20.533
That default is valuable,

5
00:00:20.613 --> 00:00:27.943
and teams should preserve it wherever
possible when displaying names,

6
00:00:28.023 --> 00:00:34.743
comments, labels, messages, and other
user-influenced values.

7
00:00:34.823 --> 00:00:43.497
Rendering markup from untrusted or weakly
trusted sources requires careful review

8
00:00:43.577 --> 00:00:45.532
and sanitization.

9
00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:53.798
A design that treats stored content as
markup has a different risk profile than a

10
00:00:53.878 --> 00:00:57.177
design that displays it as text.

11
00:00:57.257 --> 00:01:03.610
The team should know who produces the
content, who reviews it,

12
00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:10.532
how it is sanitized, and which components
are allowed to render it.

13
00:01:10.612 --> 00:01:12.444
Context matters.

14
00:01:12.524 --> 00:01:16.556
JavaScript, CSS, URL, HTML attribute,

15
00:01:16.636 --> 00:01:23.356
and markup contexts each have different
handling requirements.

16
00:01:23.436 --> 00:01:31.744
A value that is safe as ordinary text may
not be safe in another context without the

17
00:01:31.824 --> 00:01:36.222
right framework pattern or encoding path.

18
00:01:36.302 --> 00:01:44.244
Avoid mixing user-controlled values into
contexts that expect structure or

19
00:01:44.324 --> 00:01:46.645
executable behavior.

20
00:01:46.725 --> 00:01:54.544
Third-party component libraries should be
reviewed for update practices,

21
00:01:54.624 --> 00:01:59.511
data handling, and unsafe rendering
patterns.

22
00:01:59.591 --> 00:02:08.021
A component can make UI development easier
while also introducing dependencies,

23
00:02:08.101 --> 00:02:15.554
rendering behavior, and browser-side code
that the team must maintain.

24
00:02:15.634 --> 00:02:23.209
Content Security Policy can provide
defense in depth for browser-facing

25
00:02:23.289 --> 00:02:24.877
applications.

26
00:02:24.957 --> 00:02:32.532
It can help constrain some browser
behaviors when configured carefully.

27
00:02:32.612 --> 00:02:41.042
It does not replace safe rendering,
correct encoding, careful component review,

28
00:02:41.122 --> 00:02:46.743
or a disciplined approach to
user-generated content.

29
00:02:46.823 --> 00:02:52.809
The safe baseline is to favor normal
component rendering,

30
00:02:52.889 --> 00:02:57.288
limit markup rendering to reviewed cases,

31
00:02:57.368 --> 00:03:02.743
sanitize where the business requires rich
content,

32
00:03:02.823 --> 00:03:11.620
and treat browser-side rendering decisions
as part of application security review.
